# --- patch 4: a Stop that always stops -- and patch 5: steering ---------------
# Both live in the server's chat path, so they are applied together, and like
# patch 3 they land on the next server start rather than the next reload.
#
# Patch 4 (always on) fixes a hang. Stop calls the SDK's interrupt(), which is a
# control request WRITTEN TO THE CLI'S STDIN, so it is only answered while the
# CLI is alive and reading. Usually it is -- 1.37.2 holds stdin open for the
# turn, and for up to half an hour past it when the turn left background work
# running -- but a child that has already wound down, or one too busy to read,
# leaves the promise unsettled: the session is never removed (so every later
# Stop wedges on the same session), the abort handler never returns, and the
# client never receives its terminal complete. The UI sits on "processing" and
# Esc does nothing until the server is restarted. Give the control request two
# seconds, then close the transport instead -- which needs no cooperation from
# the child.
#
# Patch 5 (CLOUDCLI_STEER=1) rides that same open stdin. 1.37.2 sends every turn
# as a stream that parks when it runs out of messages (createHeldPromptStream),
# precisely so the pipe stays open. A user frame pushed into the park is read at
# the CLI's next agent-loop boundary, in the SAME turn -- so a message typed
# mid-run steers the run instead of queueing behind it. All this patch adds is a
# queue on that park and a route to reach it; nothing about how a turn is
# started has to change, which is what makes it small.
#
# (Before 1.37.2 the same trick hung off the SDK's string-prompt path, which
# left stdin open by omission, and only for turns without attachments. Upstream
# now holds it open on purpose, for every turn, and releases it on purpose --
# so steering follows a lifetime the server states rather than one it inferred,
# and a push after the release is refused rather than dropped into a closed
# pipe.)
#
# The CLI applies such a frame but never writes it down, and CloudCLI's history
# IS the CLI's transcript -- so patch 5 also appends the line there itself, in
# the shape a user turn has. Without that the message vanishes on the next
# reload, and the CLI resumes a conversation with no record of the redirection.
#
# Patch 5 is all-or-nothing across the three files by construction: a facade
# naming a function that failed to be inserted is a ReferenceError at import,
# i.e. a server that does not start. And every edit it makes is REVERSED when
# the flag is off, so the flag is the whole switch -- turning steering off is a
# restart, not a reinstall.
RTP="$PKG/dist-server/server/modules/providers/list/claude/claude-runtime.provider.js"
PRS="$PKG/dist-server/server/modules/providers/services/provider-runtime.service.js"
CWS="$PKG/dist-server/server/modules/websocket/services/chat-websocket.service.js"
